NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON COHEN V. BENEFICIAL INDUSTRIAL LOAN CORP.
1. When studying the caselaw on the Erie problem, the complexities can seem immense—but do not lose sight of the fact that most Erie questions in everyday federal court practice are easy. Indeed, many aspects of vertical choice of law in Cohen were straightforward. Which law, state or federal, would apply to the question of whether the defendant is liable or not? Which law would apply to the question of whether the plaintiff can proceed with the derivative action on behalf of the class of shareholders, rather than solely on an individual basis?

The hard Erie question was what to do about the New Jersey statute requiring plaintiffs such as Cohen to pay the defendant’s attorney’s fees if he lost and to post a bond that could cover these costs. Does this New Jersey law apply to the case in federal court? How does the Court answer this question?

2. Justices Douglas and Frankfurter, in their separate opinion, offer a definition of what would count as substance rather than procedure for purposes of Erie: laws that “define, qualify or delimit the cause of action or otherwise relate to it.” Compare this proposed scope for state law to the rule from York. Is this a clearer line? Is this a better line? Does this definition of substance do a better job defining the boundary between substance and procedure than the definition of procedure in Sibbach?

